**What was found**

- **No subexponential exact pathwidth algorithm is on record.** The best bases are 1.89 (Kitsunai et al. 2016, held) and 1.9657 (Suchan & Villanger 2009, held). Cutwidth, linear arrangement and feedback arc set are still at `O*(2^n)` with no base below 2, which Bentert et al. (ITCS 2025, held) state explicitly. Treewidth and bandwidth bases are recorded, two of them flagged "from memory, not held".
- **One ETH-conditional bound on computing a width exists, and it is linear.** Bonnet (JACM 2025, abstract on disk, paper not held) proves exact treewidth needs `2^{Ω(n)}` under ETH by a self-contained reduction from 3-SAT, and says the older chains through cutwidth or pathwidth on bounded-degree graphs gave only `2^{Ω(√n)}`. No sentence "no `2^{o(n)}` for pathwidth unless ETH fails" was found in any held paper or on-disk title, and no SETH bound on computing any width.
- **The counting method's real budget is total incidence, not clause width.** The paper computes a sum over a Boolean cube of a product of local factors. In the tensor form the exponent is in the number of wires, which is the total variable–constraint incidence, and a wide threshold constraint is allowed as one vertex of degree up to `s^{9/10}`. Fixed `k` is needed only for sparsification and the replacement-row degree. For pathwidth, a certificate of width at most `k` needs `o(n√log n)` wires, or `o(n√log n/(log log n)²)` CNF variables on the verified route. The position and nested-chain encodings are `Θ(n²)`, and any witness that determines the layout is at least `n log n` bits, so items 04, 05 and 09 must look for a different certificate object.
- **The formally verified statement carries the weaker exponent** `n(log log n)²/√log n`. The sharper bounds of Theorem 1.1 are paper-only. Item 02 must restate the verified form.
- **Kill check: not killed** on either criterion. The second criterion asks for a demonstration that the method cannot apply to any NP-hard width problem, and none exists in either direction.

